{"id": "97104976-92d3-4bfc-8395-3a4b2b934550", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:32:35.569023", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9ac6cff0-c28d-4caa-bd7d-37b5d79614fb", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'DESIGN_EOF'\n---\n\n## (h) Threats to validity & limits\n\n### h.1 — Sandbox & no-execution\n\n* **Runtime plan is paper-faithful reconstruction only in bubblewrap**; no LLM call has been executed as part of this design. Any numeric block in this document is a *specification*, never a measured outcome. To confirm an executed run, the audit manifest in §d must be filled alongside the persisted per-task CSV outputs.\n* **Network is disabled**, **GPU is absent**; therefore:\n  * No real model weights are loaded.\n  * `pip install` of `numpy` / `scipy` / `transformers` / `datasets` cannot succeed inside the bubblewrap; the statistics skeleton uses `math.erf`, `random.choices` etc. (Py3 stdlib) but the exact-Wilcoxon / Holm / `scipy.stats.wilcoxon` path is documented as **produce-locally-with-scipy path outside bubblewrap**.\n* **Storage & compute are sufficient** (24 CPU, 125 GiB RAM, 773 GiB free) for analytics in bubblewrap, but inference must run in a network-enabled environment.\n\n### h.2 — OPRO instruction-source audit (open until verified against PDF)\n\n* **OPRO-best instruction text** is taken from researcher brief and from the cited OPRO paper Table 12. The exact row-page-cell stamp **must be re-validated** at execution time: capture the paper PDF page, the table cell, and the instruction string. Until validated, the string used (\"Take a deep breath and work on this problem step-by-step.\") is treated as a *provisional placeholder* parsed from authoritative citation.\n* **OPRO top-k** — included as a level only if a published list of k best instructions exists in the OPRO paper. Per the researcher brief this is *optional*; if absent, factor X collapses to {OPRO-best, LTSBS, empty} and the H2_k hypothesis is retracted. Document this in §d's audit file before any analysis.\n* **Reproducibility of OPRO-best** — if OPRO-best were re-discovered by re-running OPRO with a different seed, the resulting instruction **could differ**. The test then measures an instruction that's a *paper artifact*, not the OPRO procedure; this is acceptable for \"OPROv1-best vs baselines\" claims but NOT equivalent to \"OPRO consistently improves over baselines\". The §d audit captures the OPRO seed/iteration provenance to express this clearly.\n\n### h.3 — BBH data / leakage audit\n\n* **BBH eval split identity** must be locked to a single GitHub tag/SHA; SHA-256 of each per-task JSONL is persisted in §d. Any task list mismatch (e.g., `dyck_languages` included or excluded — see §h.6) breaks comparability.\n* **OPRO train ↔ BBH eval overlap / contamination** is the dominant external threat. Mitigation:\n  * Confirm via public OPRO paper / repo that the OPRO train pool on these BBH tasks **does not contain BBH eval examples** (this is the claim in the OPRO paper for its BBH experiments, but must be re-asserted with reference to the OPRO release tag).\n  * If the same examples appear in both, switch the *eval* to the released BBH **eval split only** (which is disjoint from OPRO's train pool by release convention) and document the disjointness proof in §d. The execution must use exactly the BBH eval examples; OPRO-best was found on a set that does not include those examples.\n\n### h.4 — Confounds (between-condition threats)\n\n* **Instruction-length confound.** OPRO-best is longer than LTSBS. Even though the *primary* analysis reports the natural OPRO-best length (per locked §c.2), a `OPRO-best-trimmed-to-LTSBS-length` **sensitivity arm** (§g.1) is reported as a separate row. A persistent superiority of trimmed OPRO-best rules out length-as-primary-driver of any effect; failure does not refute the main claim (the claim is about OPRO-best as a single unit), but bounds interpretation.\n* **Few-shot population effect.** 0-shot is locked as the primary (§c.3). Differences in 0-shot vs 3-shot performance are *not* a confound within a single instruction; they are reported in the optional 3-shot arm only as an exploration of the prompt-format axis.\n* **Decoding stability.** `temperature=0.0` makes the comparisons deterministic per implementation, *except* for any model-side non-determinism (e.g., bfloat16 reductions on GPU). The §c.4 sensitivity arm (T=0.7, n_seeds=3) is the diagnosis; if its std is non-trivial under temperature=0 condition, the LLM client itself is non-deterministic and must be re-pinned.\n* **Parser consistency.** The **exact same parser** runs on all three instructions ever produced in §f.2. If a parser implementation mistake exists, it introduces the same bias to all three cells; the per-task McNemar between instructions is therefore robust to parser-monotone bugs. Diagnosis: a side-test against the BBH official scorer on a 50-example golden set must be recorded before claiming any p-value.\n\n### h.5 — Statistical-test limits\n\n* **Wilcoxon normal approximation** in §f.3 uses `math.erf`; for small `n` (n≤12) the approximation is poor. With 27 tasks the approximation is acceptable, but the *exact* Wilcoxon (`scipy.stats.wilcoxon`) replaces it in the production path; the normal-approx code is included only so that bubblewrap can run pre-flight checks if a paired CSV is provided.\n* **Bootstrap uses task-level resampling** — this captures uncertainty in the *task-mix mean*, not in the response-distribution-level accuracy. Per-example bootstrap over 6,511 examples would inflate ⍺-inference; we therefore publish task-level bootstrap as the headline macro-CI.\n* **MCP family choice.** For per-task tests, BH at q=0.05; for aggregates, Holm across k=2. The two stats tests use different MCP procedures by design; this is a pre-registered choice, not data-dependent.\n\n### h.6 — Open verifications (do not silently self-resolve)\n\n1. **OPRO-best row text & page/table location** in OPRO paper PDF — provisional literal above; verify cell precisely.\n2. **OPRO seed / iter_deep / scorer** when paper is silent on a value, default to the value stated as \"main experiment\" in §4 of the OPRO paper; record.\n3. **BBH GitHub release SHA** — finalize; important for SHA-256 of `/workspace/audit_files/bbh/*.jsonl` later.\n4. **BBH-eval split exact per-task N** — exact integer N per task (some tasks are 211 not 250 in published splits; confirm).\n5. **BBH official eval script** — locate upstream; if unavailable, re-derive per §f.2 and side-test against a held-out golden set; record gracefully.\n6. **Cost/fee schedule for `text-bison / PaLM 2-L`** — replace the estimate in §g.3 with the live price card before any run.\n7. **`dyck_languages` and boolean_expressions treatment** — both are part of canonical BBH 27; a mismatched list vs OPRO paper will break the macro aggregator. Our task list (§c.5) is the canonical 27 from Suzgun et al.; confirm OPRO paper Table 12 evaluates on the same 27 (or document which subset if it differs).\n8. **gpt-3.5-turbo API access** for cross-family extension. If prohibited, document and skip; do not coerce.\n\n### h.7 — Internal-validity threats (pre-registered)\n\n* Operator does not see any per-task outcome before §b's MCP rules are written; this design is a pre-registration, not a reanalysis.\n* No new statistical tests are added post-unblinding; if any are added, they are reported under \"post-hoc, not pre-registered\" in the table.\n* The decision rule in §e.7 is the only route to \"primary claim made\" / \"no claim\".\n\n### h.8 — Conclusion (limit-band statement, not result)\n\nWhat this design *can* claim after a successful mode-B run: \"Under the locked configurations of §c, on the audited BBH eval split of §d, with the decision rules of §e, the OPRO-best universal instruction is/is-not observed to outperform {LTSBS, empty} at the pre-registered acceptance thresholds.\" Anything beyond that (generalization to unseen 